## Why the wall exists

Cypress launches a runner page, then loads Bizlevate HRM in an iframe. Spec code in the top window reaches into that iframe because they share an origin relationship Cypress controls. The moment the iframe navigates to `login.okta.com`, the browser itself refuses that reach-in. Cypress did not "lose" a feature — it hit the same-origin policy every in-page script hits.

Playwright and Selenium never needed this ceremony: their test process lives outside the page and talks to the browser over CDP/WebDriver. They can read a new origin the same way they read the old one. Cypress's superpower (in-page hooks, `cy.intercept()` monkey-patches, `cy.clock()`) is the same design that makes cross-origin a special case.

## The API — url, args, callback, return

```js
const creds = { username: 'ava@bizlevate.com', password: 'correct-horse' };

cy.visit('/login');
cy.get('[data-cy=sso]').click(); // redirects to Okta

cy.origin('https://login.okta.com', { args: creds }, ({ username, password }) => {
  cy.get('#okta-signin-username').type(username);
  cy.get('#okta-signin-password').type(password);
  cy.get('#okta-signin-submit').click();
});

cy.url().should('include', '/dashboard');
cy.get('[data-cy=user-chip]').should('contain', 'Ava');
```

Signature, in order:

1. **url** — origin only matters (`https://login.okta.com`), not a full path. Cypress switches privileged access to that origin for the duration of the callback.
2. **`{ args }`** (optional) — a serializable payload cloned into the callback. There is no shared lexical scope. `password` from the outer `const creds` is invisible unless you pass it.
3. **callback** — runs as if freshly injected into that origin. Use ordinary Cypress commands against that origin's DOM.
4. **yield / return** — the last command's subject (or an explicit return of serializable data) comes back to the primary origin:

```js
cy.origin('https://login.okta.com', () => {
  cy.get('[data-se=okta-error]').invoke('text');
}).then((idpErrorText) => {
  // back on app.bizlevate.com
  expect(idpErrorText).to.match(/unable to sign in/i);
});
```

If you need more than one value, return a plain object from the callback (still serializable). Do not try to return a jQuery element or a Window.

## What you cannot do inside the callback

- Close over outer variables, imports, or `const` from the spec file.
- Pass functions, Dates-as-class, Map/Set, or DOM nodes through `args`.
- Assume `cypress/support/e2e.ts` custom commands exist automatically. In modern Cypress, call `Cypress.require('../support/commands')` (or the documented equivalent) inside the origin callback, or keep origin blocks to built-in commands.
- Treat `cy.origin` as `driver.switchTo().window()` — it is a scoped re-injection, not a handle you keep.

## chromeWebSecurity: false

Setting `chromeWebSecurity: false` in config disables Chrome's web-security checks so some cross-origin iframes become reachable. It does not work the same in Firefox, it weakens the browser you are testing, and it is the wrong default for Okta. Teach `cy.origin` first. Mention `chromeWebSecurity` only as a debugging hammer for stubborn third-party iframes, with the security caveat attached.

## Versus Playwright and Selenium

| Move | Cypress | Playwright | Selenium |
|---|---|---|---|
| App → Okta → App | `cy.origin` around IdP steps | `page.goto` / click — transparent | `get()` / click — transparent |
| Pass secrets into the foreign page | `{ args: creds }` | ordinary variables in the test process | ordinary variables |
| Read a string back | yield/return from the callback | `locator.text_content()` | `element.text` |
| Multi-origin in one test | one origin active per block | many Pages/Frames at once | many windows/frames |

Interview line: "Cypress can do SSO. It cannot pretend the IdP is same-origin. I wrap IdP interactions in `cy.origin`, pass credentials through `args`, and yield any IdP-side message back."

## What actually happens on click

```html
<a data-cy="view-payslip" href="/api/payslips/42.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
  View payslip
</a>
```

In Playwright you wait for a popup Page and assert on it. In Selenium you snapshot `getWindowHandles()` before click, diff after, `switchTo().window(newHandle)`. In Cypress the second tab may appear in the real Chrome window, but the command queue is still bound to the original iframe. There is **no** `cy.switchToTab()`. Commands after the click that look for PDF DOM will time out against the HRM page you already had.

## Workaround 1 — assert intent (often enough)

```js
cy.get('[data-cy=view-payslip]')
  .should('have.attr', 'target', '_blank')
  .and('have.attr', 'href')
  .and('match', /\/api\/payslips\/\d+\.pdf$/);
```

This is the right test when the product requirement is "this control opens the payslip resource in a new tab," not "render the PDF plugin."

## Workaround 2 — `cy.request` the same resource

```js
cy.get('[data-cy=view-payslip]').invoke('attr', 'href').then((href) => {
  cy.request({ url: href, encoding: 'binary' }).then((res) => {
    expect(res.status).to.eq(200);
    expect(res.headers['content-type']).to.match(/pdf/i);
    expect(res.body.byteLength).to.be.greaterThan(1000);
  });
});
```

You reuse the Cypress session cookies automatically on same-origin `cy.request`. That is closer to "the employee is allowed to download this payslip" than a screenshot of a PDF viewer.

## Workaround 3 — stub `window.open`

```js
cy.window().then((win) => {
  cy.stub(win, 'open').as('open');
});
cy.get('[data-cy=compare-employees]').click();
cy.get('@open').should('have.been.called');
cy.get('@open').its('args.0.0').should('include', '/employees/99');
```

You proved the app *tried* to open the URL. You did not test the other page.

## Workaround 4 — same-tab visit

Remove `target` in the test (or have the app honor a query flag) and `cy.visit` the href. Useful for HTML help articles. Awkward for binary PDFs.

## When to stop workarounding

Two-tab "mark notification read here, see badge update there" is not an href check. Playwright holds two Pages. Say so. Do not invent a Cypress plugin that "does tabs" by screenshotting the OS chrome.

Interview line: "Cypress has no switchToTab. I assert the link or request the resource. If the business process is two live windows, I use Playwright for that spec."

## Setup is a second testing type, not a flag on E2E

`npx cypress open` → Component Testing → framework wizard. Cypress detects React/Vue/Angular/Svelte and Vite/Webpack and writes a **separate** block:

```js
import { defineConfig } from 'cypress';

export default defineConfig({
  e2e: { baseUrl: 'http://localhost:3000' },
  component: {
    devServer: { framework: 'react', bundler: 'vite' },
  },
});
```

Two launchpad choices, two spec globs, two Dev Server stories. Do not point E2E `baseUrl` at a mounted component — there is no app server in CT.

## A Bizlevate example

```jsx
// src/components/LeaveRequestForm.cy.jsx
import LeaveRequestForm from './LeaveRequestForm';

describe('LeaveRequestForm', () => {
  it('keeps submit disabled until required fields are set', () => {
    cy.mount(<LeaveRequestForm employeeId={42} remainingBalance={10} />);
    cy.get('[data-cy=submit]').should('be.disabled');
    cy.get('[data-cy=leave-type]').select('Annual');
    cy.get('[data-cy=start-date]').type('2026-09-10');
    cy.get('[data-cy=end-date]').type('2026-09-12');
    cy.get('[data-cy=submit]').should('not.be.disabled');
  });

  it('warns when remainingBalance is 0', () => {
    cy.mount(<LeaveRequestForm employeeId={42} remainingBalance={0} />);
    cy.get('[data-cy=balance-warning]')
      .should('be.visible')
      .and('contain', 'No remaining leave balance');
  });

  it('calls onSubmit with the form payload', () => {
    const onSubmit = cy.stub().as('onSubmit');
    cy.mount(<LeaveRequestForm employeeId={42} remainingBalance={8} onSubmit={onSubmit} />);
    cy.get('[data-cy=leave-type]').select('Annual');
    cy.get('[data-cy=start-date]').type('2026-09-10');
    cy.get('[data-cy=end-date]').type('2026-09-12');
    cy.get('[data-cy=submit]').click();
    cy.get('@onSubmit').should('have.been.called');
  });
});
```

The zero-balance test is the teaching point: in E2E you would drain a real employee's allotment. In CT you pass a prop.

## CT vs E2E (memorize this)

| Question | Component test | E2E test |
|---|---|---|
| Does the date picker reject end < start? | Yes — cheap | Possible, slow |
| Does manager-approve update payroll? | No — no API/DB | Yes |
| Real router + auth cookies? | Mocked or absent | Real (or `cy.session`) |
| Speed | Milliseconds–seconds | Seconds–minutes |
| Failure meaning | This component, these props | Some layer in the journey |

A mature Bizlevate suite uses CT for combinatorial UI and E2E for a thin set of journeys (submit leave, approve, run payroll).

## Versus Playwright and Selenium

Playwright added component testing after Cypress made it first-class; the JS CT story exists, the Python Playwright manual you may already know is E2E-centric. Selenium drives a full browser against a deployed app — there is no `cy.mount` equivalent. If a hiring manager asks "why Cypress over Selenium for a React team?", CT is a legitimate, specific answer.

Interview line: "CT mounts one component with `cy.mount`; E2E drives the real HRM. I use CT for prop-matrix UI and E2E for integration. I do not replace one with the other."

## Install and the two-command ritual

```bash
npm install -D cypress-axe axe-core
```

```js
// cypress/support/e2e.ts
import 'cypress-axe';

it('leave modal meets axe serious+', () => {
  cy.visit('/leave');
  cy.get('[data-cy=new-request]').click();
  cy.get('[data-cy=leave-modal]').should('be.visible');

  cy.injectAxe();
  cy.checkA11y('[data-cy=leave-modal]', {
    includedImpacts: ['critical', 'serious'],
    rules: { 'color-contrast': { enabled: false } }, // tracked in JIRA-4412
  });
});
```

**Order:** the page (or modal) must exist, then `injectAxe()`, then `checkA11y()`. Injecting on a previous page and navigating away wastes the injection.

## What you get

axe reports rule ids (`label`, `button-name`, `color-contrast`, `duplicate-id`), impact, and nodes. `cy.checkA11y()` **fails the Cypress test by default** when violations remain. Playwright examples often collect violations and `expect(count).toBe(0)` manually — same engine, different default posture.

Third argument: a callback `(violations) => { cy.task('log', violations) }` if you need to log in CI without failing (short-term). Do not leave that as the permanent gate.

## Scope like a product person

Full-page axe on a dashboard with a third-party chart will drown you. Scope to `[data-cy=leave-modal]` or `main`. Disable individual rules only with a ticket id in a comment.

## Versus Playwright and Selenium

All three should use **axe-core**, not a home-grown contrast checker.

| | Cypress | Playwright | Selenium |
|---|---|---|---|
| Typical wrapper | `cypress-axe` | `@axe-core/playwright` | `axe-selenium-*` / Deque |
| Fail the test | `checkA11y()` default fail | you assert on results | you assert on results |
| Browser | whatever Cypress launched | any Playwright browser including WebKit | any WebDriver browser |

Cypress still cannot run axe in real Safari the way Playwright WebKit can. If iOS VoiceOver-class bugs are the target, axe-in-Cypress is not the whole strategy.

Interview line: "I use `cypress-axe` — same axe-core as Playwright. Inject after the UI is up, `checkA11y` scoped, fail on serious/critical. It is not a WCAG certificate."

## Locale is an app feature, not a Cypress command

```js
cy.visit('/leave?lang=ne');
// or
cy.setCookie('locale', 'ne-NP');
cy.visit('/leave');
```

Cypress does not offer Playwright's `newContext({ locale: 'de-DE', timezoneId: 'Europe/Berlin' })` because it does not manufacture isolated browser contexts that way. You launch a browser and then make the HRM app render Nepali — the same way a user would (language picker, profile, subdomain).

For `Accept-Language` at first document request:

```js
cy.visit('/leave', { headers: { 'Accept-Language': 'ne-NP,ne;q=0.9' } });
```

Headers on `cy.visit` help if the *document* is localized server-side. Client-only i18n still needs the app switch.

## Assert like a translator won't fire you

```js
cy.fixture('i18n/ne-NP.json').then((t) => {
  cy.get('[data-cy=submit]').should('have.text', t.leave.submit);
});
```

`data-cy=submit` stays stable. The fixture is the contract with the translation file. Optionally assert `html[lang="ne"]`.

Hard-coded `cy.contains('Start date')` is an English unit test in disguise.

## Dates and money

Payroll will show `Rs.` vs `$ `, and `16/09/2026` vs `9/16/2026`. Freeze time with `cy.clock(new Date('2026-09-16T00:00:00Z'))` and compare against formatted fixtures, not against `new Date()` on the runner.

CI containers should set `TZ=UTC` so screenshot/i18n tests do not drift with the GitHub runner region.

## Versus Playwright and Selenium

Playwright's context locale/timezone/geolocation is the cleanest *browser-level* i18n hook. Selenium can pass Chrome prefs (`intl.accept_languages`). Cypress sits in the middle: some visit headers, mostly application-level control. All three still need non-English locators that are not the visible string.

Interview line: "I don't assert English. I switch the app locale, use `data-cy`, and compare against translation fixtures. Cypress has no Playwright-style locale context."

## The anti-pattern

```js
it('validates leave types', () => {
  cy.fixture('leave-types.json').then((rows) => {
    rows.forEach((row) => {
      cy.get('[data-cy=leave-type]').select(row.label); // one test, six behaviors
    });
  });
});
```

One failure, useless title, one screenshot, retries repeat *all* rows.

## The pattern

Tests are registered when the spec file evaluates. Use synchronous data:

```js
const rows = require('../fixtures/leave-types.json');

describe('leave type dropdown', () => {
  rows.forEach(({ code, label }) => {
    it(`shows ${code} as ${label}`, () => {
      cy.visit('/leave/new');
      cy.get('[data-cy=leave-type]').select(label);
      cy.get('[data-cy=leave-type]').should('have.value', code);
    });
  });
});
```

Or `import rows from '../fixtures/leave-types.json'` with the right TS resolveJsonModule. If you insist on `cy.fixture`, import/require instead, or use a plugin — do not discover rows after the test started.

## Roles × journeys

```js
['employee', 'manager', 'payroll-admin'].forEach((role) => {
  it(`hides approve button for ${role} when not permitted`, () => {
    cy.loginAs(role);
    cy.visit('/leave/123');
    // ...
  });
});
```

Prefer `cy.session` inside `loginAs` so you are not paying full UI login per row (Part 7).

## Versus Playwright and Selenium

Playwright: `for (const role of roles) { test(`${role} cannot approve`, async ({ page }) => { ... }) }`. pytest: `@pytest.mark.parametrize`. TestNG: `@DataProvider`. Same rule: **the runner must see N tests**, not N loops inside one test.

Interview line: "I parametrize by generating `it()` from JSON at load time. I do not for-loop Cypress commands inside one test and call it data-driven."

## Command vs config

```js
cy.screenshot('payroll-confirm'); // cypress/screenshots/<spec>/payroll-confirm.png

cy.get('[data-cy=pay-run-card]').screenshot('pay-run-card', {
  padding: 16,
  blackout: ['[data-cy=iban]', '[data-cy=ssn]'],
  overwrite: true,
});
```

```js
// cypress.config.ts
export default defineConfig({
  screenshotOnRunFailure: true, // default true
  screenshotsFolder: 'cypress/screenshots',
  e2e: {
    setupNodeEvents(on) {
      on('after:screenshot', (details) => details);
    },
  },
});
```

Automatic failure shots do not need a command in the spec. Explicit `cy.screenshot()` is for checkpoints and documentation.

## Useful options

| Option | Why |
|---|---|
| `capture: 'fullPage' \| 'viewport' \| 'runner'` | `runner` includes the Command Log — rarely what you want in a product bug ticket |
| `blackout` | CSS selectors covered before capture — payroll PII |
| `overwrite` | stable filename for docs |
| `onAfterScreenshot` | copy/resize in Node via task if you must |

## Not visual testing

There is no threshold, no baseline, no fail-on-diff. Pair with 9.6 if you need comparison. Pair with 11.8 if you need the PNG as a CI artifact.

## Versus Playwright and Selenium

Playwright: `page.screenshot({ path })` ≈ this chapter; `expect(page).toHaveScreenshot()` ≈ 9.6. Selenium: `getScreenshotAs`. All three can leak PII in CI artifacts — blackout is an HRM-specific discipline, not a Cypress novelty.

Interview line: "`cy.screenshot()` is capture. Visual regression is a plugin. I blackout IBAN on payroll shots so artifacts are safe to store."

## Three different products people collapse into "mobile"

1. **Responsive web** — CSS at 390px. Cypress: `cy.viewport('iphone-x')`. Valid, cheap, Chromium/Firefox only.
2. **Mobile web on real iOS Safari** — WebKit + iOS chrome + real touch. Cypress: not first-class. Playwright WebKit or Sauce Safari.
3. **Native app** — `Bizlevate.app` / Play Store APK. **Appium** (or Espresso/XCUITest). Cypress has no WebView to live inside unless you wrap a web app in a browser.

If Bizlevate ships (3), Cypress skill does not transfer as "the same tests." You write new native tests. Web CT/E2E still protect the web.

## What Cypress *can* do for (1)

```js
describe('leave form on mobile width', () => {
  beforeEach(() => {
    cy.viewport('iphone-x');
    cy.visit('/leave/new');
  });
  it('does not clip the submit bar', () => {
    cy.get('[data-cy=submit]').should('be.visible');
  });
});
```

Optional: `cypress-real-events` for `realTouch`-ish behavior — still not iOS.

## Appium is the native answer

Appium drives UIAutomator2 / XCUITest over WebDriver. That is the same *external driver* philosophy as Selenium, which is why native mobile never fit Cypress's in-page model (Part 0.8). A complete automation résumé for Bizlevate is often **Cypress (web) + Appium (native)**, or Playwright web + Appium.

## Versus Playwright and Selenium

| Need | Cypress | Playwright | Selenium/Appium |
|---|---|---|---|
| Responsive web | Viewport | Viewport + devices | Chrome options |
| iOS Safari engine | No first-class WebKit | WebKit yes | SafariDriver / cloud |
| Native iOS/Android | No — use **Appium** | No — use **Appium** | **Appium** |

Interview line: "Cypress does not test native apps. Viewport is responsive web. Real iOS web needs WebKit elsewhere. Native is Appium."
